    <!-- FAQ Schema -->
    <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the basic steps of first aid?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The basic steps of first aid follow the DRSABCD approach: Danger (check for hazards), Response (check if the person responds), Send for help (call emergency services), Airway (open the airway), Breathing (check for breathing), CPR (start chest compressions if needed), and Defibrillation (use an AED if available). Always ensure your own safety first before helping others."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When should I call emergency services?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Call emergency services immediately for: unconsciousness, severe breathing difficulties, chest pain or suspected heart attack, heavy uncontrolled bleeding, suspected stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, speech difficulties), severe allergic reactions (anaphylaxis), suspected spinal injuries, seizures lasting more than 5 minutes, severe burns, or poisoning. When in doubt, always call for help - it is better to be safe than sorry."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I perform CPR on an adult?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For adult CPR: 1) Call emergency services first. 2) Place the person on their back on a firm surface. 3) Place the heel of one hand on the center of the chest, other hand on top. 4) Push hard and fast at a rate of 100-120 compressions per minute, at least 5cm deep. 5) If trained, give 2 rescue breaths after every 30 compressions. 6) Continue until emergency services arrive or the person starts breathing. Use an AED if available."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I treat a burn injury?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For burns: 1) Remove the person from the heat source safely. 2) Cool the burn under cool (not cold) running water for at least 20 minutes. 3) Remove jewelry and clothing near the burn unless stuck to the skin. 4) Cover with a clean, non-fluffy dressing or cling film. 5) Do NOT apply ice, butter, toothpaste, or any creams. 6) Seek medical attention for burns larger than your palm, on the face/hands/feet/genitals, or if they appear deep (white or charred)."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What should I do if someone is choking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a choking adult: 1) Encourage them to cough if they can. 2) If they cannot cough, speak, or breathe: stand behind them, lean them forward. 3) Give up to 5 back blows between the shoulder blades. 4) If unsuccessful, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich maneuver): place your fist above the navel, grasp with other hand, and thrust inward and upward. 5) Alternate between back blows and abdominal thrusts. 6) If they become unconscious, start CPR and call emergency services."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I stop severe bleeding?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "To stop severe bleeding: 1) Call emergency services. 2) Apply direct pressure using a clean cloth or bandage. 3) Keep constant pressure for at least 10-15 minutes without lifting to check. 4) If blood soaks through, add more material on top without removing the first layer. 5) If possible, elevate the injured limb above the heart. 6) For life-threatening bleeding, consider using a tourniquet above the wound if trained to do so. 7) Keep the person warm and calm until help arrives."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    </script>

